<!--
  Momo - a 1.43 m companion robot for the home environment.

  Why this exists as a separate model from pyunto_h1:

    * Proportioned for a household rather than an office. The tasks here are at counter height
      and inside a drum washer, so the arm has to reach DOWN and IN, not up at a door handle.
    * Two-finger grippers sized for fabric, not for a 3.6 cm door handle. Fabric is grasped by
      welding a cloth vertex to the palm (see brain/laundry.py), the same mechanism the office
      robot uses for door handles, so the finger geometry only has to look right and provide
      the contact that triggers the grasp.
    * An appearance that reads as a character rather than as industrial hardware. That is a
      product requirement, not decoration: this robot is the face of a consumer app, and the
      one thing a user sees before anything else is what it looks like.

  ============================ ORIENTATION: READ THIS FIRST ============================

  The robot faces +x. The face, the apron, the collar ribbon and head_cam all point that way.

  They did not originally: the face was modelled looking along -y while head_cam looked along
  +x, so the robot walked sideways relative to its own face and every photograph caught it in
  profile. When adding anything to the front of this model -- a face feature, a hair strand, a
  garment detail -- the forward offset goes in x, never in y.

  ================================= THE APPEARANCE =================================

  Everything here is MuJoCo primitives: spheres, capsules, boxes, ellipsoids. There are no
  meshes and nothing is loaded from disk. The target is a Japanese anime character, and out of
  primitives that comes down to a handful of specific decisions:

    * PROPORTION. Head 0.112 m in half-width on a 1.43 m body, roughly 1:6 head-to-height
      against about 1:7.5 for an adult. The skull is narrow front-to-back and wide across,
      because an anime head gets its width from hair rather than bone.

    * A TAPERED JAW. Wide at the temples, narrowing to a small chin. This is the single most
      characteristic thing about the face type: a rounded jaw reads as a doll or a baby, and
      the taper is what reads as a character.

    * LAYERED EYES, and they are the whole design. Each is a stack -- pale sclera, large
      saturated iris, a brighter ring low in the iris, a dark pupil, and two asymmetric
      catchlights -- capped by a dark lash. That layering is why they look alive rather than
      painted on, and it is worth the geom count. Half-height 0.040 against a 0.120 skull
      makes each eye a third of the face tall, which is far past human and exactly right.

    * A SHORT FOREHEAD. The fringe comes down to just above the brows. Cut higher, the expanse
      of bare skin between brow and hairline was the one thing making the face read as odd.

    * POINTED HAIR CLUMPS, not a smooth helmet. Every strand tapers, and the shape comes from
      a fringe of pointed locks, side locks framing the face, twintails sweeping back, and one
      stray strand standing up (the "ahoge"). The ahoge is the cheapest geometry in the model
      and does more than any of it to make the character look drawn rather than moulded.

    * ROUND JOINTS. Shoulders, hands, knees and hips are spheres; limbs are capsules. There is
      not a sharp exposed corner above the ankles.

  Two traps worth knowing, because both cost time:

    * MuJoCo renders SITES as solid spheres. The head_cam marker sits between the eyes, so the
      default red debug site rendered as a bright red dot on the bridge of the nose -- and it
      survived two rounds of hunting for a stray nose geom that had already been removed. Both
      it and the gripper sites are alpha 0 here; the names are still used by code.

    * A default-class `rgba` silently overrides every geom's `material`, and the whole robot
      renders bone white. Defaults set physics; materials set appearance.

  Colour is also functional. The hair is a saturated pink that appears nowhere else in the
  house scene, so ColorGrounder can pick the robot out of a frame; likewise the mint uniform.
  See perception/grounding.py _PALETTE.

  Joint budget (24 actuated DoF), same philosophy as H1 - fewer joints is easier to control:
    legs 2x6 = 12   hip yaw/roll/pitch, knee, ankle pitch/roll
    waist       1   yaw
    neck        2   yaw + pitch (pitch matters here: laundry is below eye level)
    arms 2x4 = 8    shoulder pitch/roll/yaw, elbow
    grippers 2x1    one actuator per hand, fingers coupled by a tendon

  The neck PITCH is the one addition over H1 and it is load-bearing: everything Momo works
  with - the drum opening, the basket, a towel on a counter - is below eye level, and a head
  that can only pan left and right cannot look down into any of them.
-->
<mujoco model="momo">
  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <default>
    <default class="momo">
      <!--
        NOTE: no rgba here on purpose. A default-class rgba OVERRIDES a geom's material and
        the whole robot renders bone white -- every colour below is set through `material`,
        so a stray default rgba silently throws all of it away. Defaults set physics; the
        materials set appearance.
      -->
      <geom type="capsule" condim="3" friction="0.9 0.05 0.01" density="800" group="1"/>
      <joint type="hinge" damping="2" armature="0.02" limited="true"/>
      <position kp="120" dampratio="1" ctrllimited="true"/>

      <default class="m_leg">
        <joint damping="5" armature="0.05"/>
        <position kp="400" forcerange="-200 200"/>
      </default>
      <!--
        Arm gain is 500, not H1's 150. H1 only ever holds its arm out briefly to shove a door,
        and a servo that sags a little while doing it costs nothing. Momo has to put a hand ON
        a specific cloth corner, and at kp=150 the shoulder settled 0.20 rad below its command
        under the arm's own weight -- which put the gripper 0.19 m from a target the IK had
        solved to 0.02 m. The error was gravity droop, not bad IK, and no amount of solving
        fixes a servo that cannot hold the pose it is given.

        dampratio="1" keeps it critically damped, so the stiffer joint does not overshoot.
      -->
      <default class="m_arm">
        <joint damping="2" armature="0.02"/>
        <position kp="500" dampratio="1" forcerange="-120 120"/>
      </default>
      <default class="m_finger">
        <!--
          Fingers are soft and grippy. condim=4 plus high friction is what lets a pinch hold
          light objects at all; anything heavy is held by the weld, as with the office doors.
        -->
        <joint damping="0.3" armature="0.005"/>
        <geom material="skin" friction="2.2 0.1 0.01" condim="4"/>
        <position kp="30" forcerange="-25 25"/>
      </default>
      <default class="m_foot">
        <geom type="box" friction="1.5 0.1 0.01" condim="6" material="shoe"/>
      </default>
      <!--
        Decoration: seen by the camera, never touched by the solver. contype/conaffinity 0
        keeps hair and eyes out of collision detection entirely, so a 40-sphere hairstyle
        costs nothing in the physics loop. Density 1 so it barely perturbs the inertia either.
      -->
      <default class="deco">
        <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0" density="1" group="1"/>
      </default>
    </default>
  </default>

  <asset>
    <!--
      Skin is warmer and a shade deeper than it looks it should be on paper. At 0.99/0.91/0.88
      the strong key light blew it out to flat white and the face lost all its modelling.
    -->
    <material name="skin"      rgba="0.98 0.84 0.78 1" specular="0.18" shininess="0.25"/>
    <material name="hair"      rgba="0.98 0.55 0.72 1" specular="0.45" shininess="0.55"/>
    <material name="hair_dark" rgba="0.90 0.42 0.62 1" specular="0.45" shininess="0.55"/>
    <material name="dress"     rgba="0.55 0.90 0.83 1" specular="0.30" shininess="0.40"/>
    <material name="dress_alt" rgba="0.42 0.80 0.75 1" specular="0.30"/>
    <material name="apron"     rgba="0.99 0.99 0.99 1" specular="0.20"/>
    <material name="eye_white" rgba="0.99 0.99 0.99 1" specular="0.60" shininess="0.80"/>
    <!--
      The iris is layered, which is how anime eyes are drawn: a deep saturated body with a
      brighter ring low down, so the eye looks translucent and lit from below rather than flat.
    -->
    <material name="eye_iris"  rgba="0.16 0.48 0.80 1" specular="0.80" shininess="0.90"/>
    <material name="eye_glow"  rgba="0.48 0.82 0.97 1" specular="0.85" shininess="0.95"/>
    <material name="eye_pupil" rgba="0.07 0.10 0.20 1" specular="0.70" shininess="0.85"/>
    <!-- Lashes are near-black with a warm cast, never pure black, which reads as harsh. -->
    <material name="lash_mat"  rgba="0.16 0.10 0.18 1" specular="0.35" shininess="0.40"/>
    <material name="blush"     rgba="0.99 0.68 0.70 1" specular="0.15"/>
    <material name="mouth"     rgba="0.90 0.45 0.50 1" specular="0.30"/>
    <material name="joint_mat" rgba="0.88 0.86 0.92 1" specular="0.40" shininess="0.50"/>
    <material name="shoe"      rgba="0.42 0.34 0.55 1" specular="0.35"/>
    <material name="ribbon"    rgba="0.99 0.42 0.58 1" specular="0.50" shininess="0.60"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!--
      Pelvis is the floating base, as on H1. 0.7496 puts the soles on z=0 with the "stand"
      keyframe's bent knees; it is solved from the leg chain, not guessed.
    -->
    <body name="pelvis" pos="0 0 0.7659" childclass="momo">
      <freejoint name="root"/>
      <inertial pos="0 0 0" mass="5.0" diaginertia="0.05 0.045 0.035"/>
      <geom name="pelvis_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.105 0.115 0.078" material="dress"/>

      <!--
        Skirt: a short flared shape below the waist. Purely visual, and the reason the lower
        body silhouettes as a character rather than as two bare legs on a box.

        It is deliberately WIDE relative to the legs (0.19 against a 0.056 thigh). A narrow
        skirt over long legs reads as a spindly doll; a wide one gives the lower body an
        actual shape and shortens the apparent leg, which is what the proportion needs.
      -->
      <geom name="skirt_g" type="cylinder" size="0.150 0.062" pos="0 0 -0.052"
            material="dress" class="deco"/>
      <geom name="skirt_hem" type="cylinder" size="0.196 0.022" pos="0 0 -0.100"
            material="dress_alt" class="deco"/>

      <!-- ================= TORSO ================= -->
      <body name="torso" pos="0 0 0.100">
        <joint name="waist_yaw" axis="0 0 1" range="-1.0 1.0"/>
        <inertial pos="0 0 0.12" mass="9.0" diaginertia="0.14 0.12 0.07"/>
        <!--
          Ellipsoid rather than a box: no hard corners anywhere on the silhouette. Wide and
          SHORT (0.125 wide against 0.135 tall) -- a narrow tall torso between a big head and
          a wide skirt leaves a pinched waist that reads as fragile rather than cute.
        -->
        <geom name="torso_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.128 0.135 0.158" pos="0 0 0.132"
              material="dress"/>
        <!-- Apron/pinafore front panel, and a collar. Both decoration. -->
        <!--
          The pinafore front, on the robot's FRONT (+x) like the face. It used to sit on -y,
          matching the old head, so the robot wore its apron on one hip.
        -->
        <!--
          Pinafore: a curved bib rather than a flat rectangle. A box on the chest rendered as a
          plain white square with a shadow in the middle -- a sign hung round the neck, not an
          apron. Two ellipsoids that follow the torso's own curve read as cloth lying on it.
        -->
        <geom name="apron_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.020 0.070 0.076" pos="0.112 0 0.104"
              material="apron" class="deco"/>
        <geom name="apron_top" type="ellipsoid" size="0.016 0.040 0.030" pos="0.114 0 0.186"
              material="apron" class="deco"/>
        <!-- shoulder straps, so the pinafore is held up by something -->
        <geom name="strap_l" type="capsule" fromto="0.096 0.036 0.196 0.036 0.092 0.246"
              size="0.011" material="apron" class="deco"/>
        <geom name="strap_r" type="capsule" fromto="0.096 -0.036 0.196 0.036 -0.092 0.246"
              size="0.011" material="apron" class="deco"/>
        <!-- a waist frill where the pinafore meets the skirt -->
        <geom name="apron_hem" type="ellipsoid" size="0.024 0.084 0.016" pos="0.104 0 0.028"
              material="dress_alt" class="deco"/>
        <!--
          Collar sits at the very top of the torso, BELOW the neck stub. Any higher and it
          intersects the chin, which from the front looks like the robot is wearing a bowl.
        -->
        <geom name="collar_g" type="cylinder" size="0.076 0.014" pos="0 0 0.262"
              material="apron" class="deco"/>
        <!-- Collar ribbon, at the throat on the front (+x). -->
        <geom name="ribbon_c" type="ellipsoid" size="0.020 0.038 0.024" pos="0.058 0 0.258"
              material="ribbon" class="deco"/>

        <!-- ================= HEAD ================= -->
        <!--
          The head faces +x, which is the robot's forward axis and where head_cam looks.

          It did not, originally: the face was modelled looking along -y while the camera
          looked along +x, so the robot walked sideways relative to its own face and every
          photograph caught it in profile. Nothing about the body needed to change -- only the
          face was wrong -- but "only the face" is the whole point of this model, so the entire
          head is laid out here in one consistent frame:

              +x  forward, out of the face
              +y  the robot's LEFT
              +z  up

          Every face and hair geom below follows it. When adding one, put the forward offset in
          x, never in y.
        -->
        <body name="head" pos="0 0 0.340">
          <inertial pos="0 0 0.06" mass="1.4" diaginertia="0.008 0.008 0.007"/>
          <!--
            Two neck joints. Yaw is the same idea as H1's: the body can steer around an
            obstacle while the eyes stay on the target. Pitch is new and is what makes this
            robot able to do laundry at all - the drum opening, the basket and the counter are
            all below eye level, and a pan-only head cannot look down into any of them.
          -->
          <joint name="neck_yaw" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" pos="0 0 -0.052"
                 range="-1.75 1.75" damping="1.5" stiffness="0" armature="0.005"/>
          <joint name="neck_pitch" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" pos="0 0 -0.052"
                 range="-0.35 1.05" damping="1.5" stiffness="0" armature="0.005"/>
          <geom name="neck_g" type="cylinder" size="0.028 0.028" pos="0 0 -0.052"
                material="skin"/>

          <!--
            Skull. Narrow in x (0.100) and wide in y (0.112): an anime head is fairly flat
            front-to-back and its width comes from the hair, not the skull. Slightly taller
            than wide, which reads better than a sphere.
          -->
          <geom name="head_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.100 0.112 0.120" pos="0 0 0.018"
                material="skin"/>
          <!--
            Jaw and chin. Two tapering ellipsoids narrowing toward a point below and forward,
            which is the single most characteristic thing about an anime face: the head is
            wide at the temples and comes down to a small pointed chin. A rounded jaw reads as
            a doll or a baby; the taper is what reads as a character.
          -->
          <geom name="jaw_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.072 0.078 0.046" pos="0.014 0 -0.044"
                material="skin" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="chin_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.034 0.036 0.026" pos="0.024 0 -0.080"
                material="skin" class="deco"/>

          <!-- ============================ FACE ============================
               Everything here is deco: no collisions, negligible mass.

               The eyes are the whole design. Anime eyes are LARGE, TALL and set LOW and WIDE,
               and they are built from stacked layers: a pale sclera, a big saturated iris, a
               dark pupil, a bright ring low in the iris, and two catchlights. That layering is
               why they look alive rather than painted, and it is worth the geom count.

               Half-height 0.042 against a 0.120 skull makes each eye a third of the face tall.
               That is far past human and exactly right here.
          -->
          <!-- sclera: tall ovals, angled slightly so the outer corner sits higher -->
          <geom name="eye_l_w" type="ellipsoid" size="0.019 0.030 0.040" pos="0.080 0.039 0.018"
                material="eye_white" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="eye_r_w" type="ellipsoid" size="0.019 0.030 0.040" pos="0.080 -0.039 0.018"
                material="eye_white" class="deco"/>
          <!-- iris: large, filling most of the sclera -->
          <geom name="eye_l_i" type="ellipsoid" size="0.013 0.023 0.031" pos="0.090 0.039 0.015"
                material="eye_iris" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="eye_r_i" type="ellipsoid" size="0.013 0.023 0.031" pos="0.090 -0.039 0.015"
                material="eye_iris" class="deco"/>
          <!--
            A brighter ring low in the iris. Anime eyes are almost always drawn darker at the
            top and lighter at the bottom, which is what suggests a translucent iris catching
            light from below.
          -->
          <geom name="eye_l_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.010 0.018 0.015" pos="0.095 0.039 0.001"
                material="eye_glow" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="eye_r_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.010 0.018 0.015" pos="0.095 -0.039 0.001"
                material="eye_glow" class="deco"/>
          <!-- pupil -->
          <geom name="eye_l_p" type="ellipsoid" size="0.008 0.011 0.018" pos="0.097 0.039 0.016"
                material="eye_pupil" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="eye_r_p" type="ellipsoid" size="0.008 0.011 0.018" pos="0.097 -0.039 0.016"
                material="eye_pupil" class="deco"/>
          <!--
            Catchlights: one large high and outboard, one small low and inboard. Two of them,
            asymmetric, is the convention -- a single centred highlight looks like plastic.
          -->
          <geom name="eye_l_h1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.008 0.011 0.013" pos="0.101 0.047 0.035"
                material="eye_white" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="eye_r_h1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.008 0.011 0.013" pos="0.101 -0.031 0.035"
                material="eye_white" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="eye_l_h2" type="ellipsoid" size="0.006 0.007 0.008" pos="0.101 0.031 -0.003"
                material="eye_white" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="eye_r_h2" type="ellipsoid" size="0.006 0.007 0.008" pos="0.101 -0.047 -0.003"
                material="eye_white" class="deco"/>
          <!--
            Upper lash: a dark lid capping each eye, tilted so the outer corner lifts. This is
            drawn as a thick dark line in every anime style and is what gives the eye its
            shape.

            Two earlier attempts at a lash BAR failed -- they rendered as heavy black eyebrows
            floating above the face, because a thin box tucked against a stack of ellipsoids
            loses the depth fight at most angles. An ellipsoid slightly wider than the sclera,
            sunk into its top, sits on the eye instead of hovering over it.
          -->
          <geom name="lash_l" type="ellipsoid" size="0.018 0.032 0.011" pos="0.081 0.039 0.054"
                material="lash_mat" euler="0.20 0 0" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="lash_r" type="ellipsoid" size="0.018 0.032 0.011" pos="0.081 -0.039 0.054"
                material="lash_mat" euler="-0.20 0 0" class="deco"/>
          <!-- outer lash tips, angled up: the little flick at the corner of the eye -->
          <geom name="lash_l_tip" type="ellipsoid" size="0.012 0.014 0.008"
                pos="0.072 0.068 0.058" material="lash_mat" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="lash_r_tip" type="ellipsoid" size="0.012 0.014 0.008"
                pos="0.072 -0.068 0.058" material="lash_mat" class="deco"/>
          <!--
            Eyebrows, tucked just under the fringe rather than above it.

            At z=0.082 they sat on the hairline and rendered as two pink marks floating on the
            fringe -- reading as part of the hair, not as brows. Lowered to 0.062 and pushed
            forward to x=0.092 they sit on the forehead in the gap the fringe leaves, which is
            where a drawn brow goes. Kept thin and soft: a heavy brow on this face reads cross.
          -->
          <geom name="brow_l" type="ellipsoid" size="0.006 0.023 0.004" pos="0.092 0.039 0.062"
                material="hair_dark" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="brow_r" type="ellipsoid" size="0.006 0.023 0.004" pos="0.092 -0.039 0.062"
                material="hair_dark" class="deco"/>
          <!--
            Blush: flat ovals on the cheeks BELOW the eyes, sunk into the face rather than
            standing off it (half-depth 0.004 against 0.012 before). Placed level with the eyes
            and proud of the surface, they rendered as two pink lumps growing out of the
            cheekbones and reading as part of the eye rather than as colour on skin.
          -->
          <geom name="blush_l" type="ellipsoid" size="0.004 0.022 0.011" pos="0.074 0.052 -0.046"
                material="blush" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="blush_r" type="ellipsoid" size="0.004 0.022 0.011" pos="0.074 -0.052 -0.046"
                material="blush" class="deco"/>
          <!--
            A small mouth, low on the jaw. Its height is not free: with the eyes centred at
            z=+0.018 a mouth at -0.070 sits barely below them and reads as a red nose between
            the eyes rather than as a mouth. On the jaw at -0.104 it reads correctly, and the
            long gap between eyes and mouth is itself an anime convention.
          -->
          <geom name="mouth_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.007 0.014 0.006" pos="0.064 0 -0.072"
                material="mouth" class="deco"/>
          <!--
            No nose geom. An anime face draws the nose as a single short stroke or omits it
            entirely, and a small ellipsoid sitting proud of the face rendered as a bright dot
            between the eyes -- a clown nose, not a character.
          -->

          <!-- ============================ HAIR ============================
               Anime hair is drawn as separate POINTED CLUMPS, not as a smooth helmet, and that
               is the difference between this and the first version. Every strand below tapers:
               a capsule or a stretched ellipsoid with its narrow end outward.

               Three groups: a crown shell, a fringe of pointed locks over the forehead, and
               long side locks framing the face -- plus twintails, which do most of the work of
               making the silhouette recognisable from any angle.
          -->
          <!-- crown: sits back and above the skull, capping it rather than swallowing it -->
          <geom name="hair_top1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.108 0.120 0.104" pos="-0.012 0 0.046"
                material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="hair_top2" type="ellipsoid" size="0.086 0.100 0.070" pos="-0.030 0 0.076"
                material="hair" class="deco"/>

          <!--
            Fringe. Six pointed clumps across the forehead, each a capsule running down-and-
            forward so it ends in a point just above the brows.

            The tips reach z=0.024-0.046, only a little above the brows at 0.082, and that is
            deliberate: an anime face has a SHORT forehead. Cut higher, the expanse of bare
            skin between brow and hairline was the single thing making the face read as odd
            rather than as drawn.
          -->
          <geom name="fringe_1" type="capsule" fromto="0.028 0.020 0.108 0.078 0.028 0.046"
                size="0.018" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="fringe_2" type="capsule" fromto="0.028 -0.020 0.108 0.078 -0.028 0.046"
                size="0.018" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="fringe_3" type="capsule" fromto="0.020 0.058 0.106 0.068 0.070 0.038"
                size="0.018" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="fringe_4" type="capsule" fromto="0.020 -0.058 0.106 0.068 -0.070 0.038"
                size="0.018" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="fringe_5" type="capsule" fromto="0.006 0.092 0.102 0.046 0.104 0.024"
                size="0.017" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="fringe_6" type="capsule" fromto="0.006 -0.092 0.102 0.046 -0.104 0.024"
                size="0.017" material="hair" class="deco"/>

          <!--
            Side locks: long tapering strands down either side of the face, ending in a point
            below the chin. These frame the face and are the other half of what stops the head
            reading as a helmet.
          -->
          <geom name="lock_l1" type="capsule" fromto="0.010 0.106 0.040 0.030 0.104 -0.090"
                size="0.023" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="lock_r1" type="capsule" fromto="0.010 -0.106 0.040 0.030 -0.104 -0.090"
                size="0.023" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="lock_l2" type="capsule" fromto="0.030 0.102 -0.090 0.040 0.096 -0.150"
                size="0.019" material="hair_dark" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="lock_r2" type="capsule" fromto="0.030 -0.102 -0.090 0.040 -0.096 -0.150"
                size="0.019" material="hair_dark" class="deco"/>

          <!-- back of the head, so the silhouette is full from behind too -->
          <geom name="hair_back1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.098 0.118 0.112" pos="-0.052 0 -0.004"
                material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="hair_back2" type="ellipsoid" size="0.072 0.092 0.080" pos="-0.062 0 -0.090"
                material="hair_dark" class="deco"/>

          <!--
            Twintails. Two bunches gathered high at the sides and falling outward and back,
            each built from three shrinking segments so it tapers to a point.

            These are the most recognisable thing about the silhouette and the reason the robot
            reads as a character from behind or in profile, not only face on.

            They sweep BACK (x from -0.052 out to -0.140), not out to the side. Hanging level
            with the head they fell forward over the shoulders in profile and made the whole
            head look tilted down, when the neck was in fact perfectly level -- an illusion
            created entirely by where the hair mass sat.
          -->
          <geom name="tail_l1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.044 0.042 0.048" pos="-0.052 0.112 0.066"
                material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="tail_l2" type="capsule" fromto="-0.064 0.124 0.046 -0.112 0.142 -0.062"
                size="0.034" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="tail_l3" type="capsule" fromto="-0.112 0.142 -0.062 -0.140 0.150 -0.152"
                size="0.021" material="hair_dark" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="tail_r1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.044 0.042 0.048" pos="-0.052 -0.112 0.066"
                material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="tail_r2" type="capsule" fromto="-0.064 -0.124 0.046 -0.112 -0.142 -0.062"
                size="0.034" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="tail_r3" type="capsule" fromto="-0.112 -0.142 -0.062 -0.140 -0.150 -0.152"
                size="0.021" material="hair_dark" class="deco"/>

          <!--
            Ahoge: the single strand standing up off the crown. Three shrinking segments in an
            arc, curling forward. It is the cheapest geometry in the whole model and does more
            than any of it to make the character look drawn rather than moulded.
          -->
          <geom name="ahoge_1" type="capsule" fromto="-0.020 0.006 0.140 0.004 0.010 0.186"
                size="0.011" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="ahoge_2" type="capsule" fromto="0.004 0.010 0.186 0.040 0.014 0.202"
                size="0.008" material="hair" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="ahoge_3" type="capsule" fromto="0.040 0.014 0.202 0.066 0.016 0.192"
                size="0.005" material="hair" class="deco"/>

          <!-- ribbons tying the twintails -->
          <geom name="ribbon_l1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.020 0.028 0.026" pos="-0.048 0.106 0.098"
                material="ribbon" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="ribbon_l2" type="ellipsoid" size="0.014 0.030 0.020" pos="-0.060 0.132 0.106"
                material="ribbon" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="ribbon_r1" type="ellipsoid" size="0.020 0.028 0.026" pos="-0.048 -0.106 0.098"
                material="ribbon" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="ribbon_r2" type="ellipsoid" size="0.014 0.030 0.020" pos="-0.060 -0.132 0.106"
                material="ribbon" class="deco"/>

          <!--
            ============================ CAMERAS ============================
            head_cam looks along +x, which is now the direction the face points. The two of
            them agreeing is the whole reason this block was rewritten: before, the camera
            looked forward while the face looked 90 degrees to the side.

            Mounted between and just in front of the eyes, which is where a character's line of
            sight comes from.
          -->
          <camera name="head_cam" pos="0.105 0 0.000" xyaxes="0 -1 0 0 0 1" fovy="75"/>
          <!--
            The camera marker is INVISIBLE (alpha 0), and that matters more here than on the
            other robots. MuJoCo renders sites as solid spheres, and on this face the camera
            sits exactly between the eyes -- so the default red debug marker rendered as a
            bright red dot on the bridge of the nose. It looked like a clown nose, and it
            survived two rounds of looking for a stray nose geom that had already been removed.

            It is kept as a site rather than deleted because code addresses it by name.
          -->
          <site name="head_cam_site" pos="0.105 0 0.000" size="0.006" rgba="1 0 0 0"/>
          <camera name="look_left" pos="0.070 0.050 0.000" xyaxes="0.866 -0.5 0 0 0 1" fovy="90"/>
          <camera name="look_right" pos="0.070 -0.050 0.000" xyaxes="-0.866 -0.5 0 0 0 1" fovy="90"/>
        </body>

        <!-- ================= ARMS =================
             Orthogonal shoulder axes (pitch/roll/yaw), same reasoning as H1: skewed axes make
             IK painful. Rounded shoulder and elbow caps keep the silhouette soft.
        -->
        <!--
          Arms are short and thick on purpose: 0.175 upper + 0.155 fore, at radius 0.040.
          Long thin limbs on a big head read as a spindly puppet -- the head has to stay the
          dominant mass, and stubby limbs are what keeps it that way.
        -->
        <body name="shoulder_r" pos="0 -0.150 0.190">
          <joint name="sh_pitch_r" axis="0 1 0" range="-3.1 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
          <joint name="sh_roll_r" axis="1 0 0" range="-1.7 0.35" class="m_arm"/>
          <joint name="sh_yaw_r" axis="0 0 1" range="-1.6 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
          <inertial pos="0 0 -0.09" mass="1.1" diaginertia="0.008 0.008 0.003"/>
          <geom name="sh_cap_r" type="sphere" size="0.056" material="dress"/>
          <!-- puff sleeve -->
          <geom name="sleeve_r" type="ellipsoid" size="0.062 0.062 0.052" pos="0 0 -0.042"
                material="dress" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="uarm_r" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.050 0 0 -0.175" size="0.040"
                material="skin"/>

          <body name="forearm_r" pos="0 0 -0.175">
            <joint name="elbow_r" axis="0 1 0" range="-2.4 0.0" class="m_arm"/>
            <inertial pos="0 0 -0.08" mass="0.7" diaginertia="0.004 0.004 0.0015"/>
            <geom name="elb_cap_r" type="sphere" size="0.040" material="joint_mat"/>
            <geom name="farm_r" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.028 0 0 -0.155" size="0.036"
                  material="skin"/>

            <!-- Hand: a rounded palm plus two soft fingers, coupled by a tendon. -->
            <body name="palm_r" pos="0 0 -0.175">
              <inertial pos="0 0 -0.02" mass="0.20" diaginertia="0.0003 0.0003 0.0002"/>
              <geom name="palm_r_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.026 0.040 0.028" material="skin"/>
              <!-- invisible: code uses this site, but a green dot on the hand is not wanted -->
              <site name="grip_r" pos="0 0 -0.058" size="0.010" rgba="0 1 0 0"/>
              <body name="finger_r1" pos="0 -0.030 -0.020">
                <joint name="grip_r1" axis="1 0 0" range="0 0.9" class="m_finger"/>
                <geom name="fing_r1" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.062" size="0.015"
                      class="m_finger"/>
              </body>
              <body name="finger_r2" pos="0 0.030 -0.020">
                <joint name="grip_r2" axis="-1 0 0" range="0 0.9" class="m_finger"/>
                <geom name="fing_r2" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.062" size="0.015"
                      class="m_finger"/>
              </body>
            </body>
          </body>
        </body>

        <body name="shoulder_l" pos="0 0.150 0.190">
          <joint name="sh_pitch_l" axis="0 1 0" range="-3.1 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
          <joint name="sh_roll_l" axis="1 0 0" range="-0.35 1.7" class="m_arm"/>
          <joint name="sh_yaw_l" axis="0 0 1" range="-1.6 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
          <inertial pos="0 0 -0.09" mass="1.1" diaginertia="0.008 0.008 0.003"/>
          <geom name="sh_cap_l" type="sphere" size="0.056" material="dress"/>
          <geom name="sleeve_l" type="ellipsoid" size="0.062 0.062 0.052" pos="0 0 -0.042"
                material="dress" class="deco"/>
          <geom name="uarm_l" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.050 0 0 -0.175" size="0.040"
                material="skin"/>

          <body name="forearm_l" pos="0 0 -0.175">
            <joint name="elbow_l" axis="0 1 0" range="-2.4 0.0" class="m_arm"/>
            <inertial pos="0 0 -0.08" mass="0.7" diaginertia="0.004 0.004 0.0015"/>
            <geom name="elb_cap_l" type="sphere" size="0.040" material="joint_mat"/>
            <geom name="farm_l" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.028 0 0 -0.155" size="0.036"
                  material="skin"/>

            <body name="palm_l" pos="0 0 -0.175">
              <inertial pos="0 0 -0.02" mass="0.20" diaginertia="0.0003 0.0003 0.0002"/>
              <geom name="palm_l_g" type="ellipsoid" size="0.026 0.040 0.028" material="skin"/>
              <!-- invisible: code uses this site, but a green dot on the hand is not wanted -->
              <site name="grip_l" pos="0 0 -0.058" size="0.010" rgba="0 1 0 0"/>
              <body name="finger_l1" pos="0 -0.030 -0.020">
                <joint name="grip_l1" axis="1 0 0" range="0 0.9" class="m_finger"/>
                <geom name="fing_l1" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.062" size="0.015"
                      class="m_finger"/>
              </body>
              <body name="finger_l2" pos="0 0.030 -0.020">
                <joint name="grip_l2" axis="-1 0 0" range="0 0.9" class="m_finger"/>
                <geom name="fing_l2" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 -0.062" size="0.015"
                      class="m_finger"/>
              </body>
            </body>
          </body>
        </body>
      </body>

      <!-- ================= LEGS =================
           Hip order yaw -> roll -> pitch, as on H1. Rounded hip and knee caps; the shoes are
           boxes because feet need flat, predictable ground contact.
      -->
      <!--
        Legs: SHORT and thick, and set wide (0.105 either side of centre). Three things were
        wrong with the first attempt and all of them were proportion, not physics: legs 0.66 m
        long under a 0.30 m body read as stilts; at radius 0.048 they were thinner than the
        arms; and at 0.082 apart they overlapped into a single column from the front.

        0.245 thigh + 0.235 shin keeps the hip at a plausible height for a 1.37 m robot while
        leaving the head the dominant mass in the silhouette.
      -->
      <body name="hip_r" pos="0 -0.105 -0.052">
        <joint name="hip_yaw_r" axis="0 0 1" range="-0.7 0.7" class="m_leg"/>
        <joint name="hip_roll_r" axis="1 0 0" range="-0.5 0.5" class="m_leg"/>
        <joint name="hip_pitch_r" axis="0 1 0" range="-1.9 1.0" class="m_leg"/>
        <inertial pos="0 0 -0.12" mass="3.2" diaginertia="0.022 0.022 0.006"/>
        <geom name="hip_cap_r" type="sphere" size="0.060" material="dress"/>
        <geom name="thigh_r" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.045 0 0 -0.345" size="0.058"
              material="skin"/>

        <body name="shin_r" pos="0 0 -0.345">
          <joint name="knee_r" axis="0 1 0" range="0 2.2" class="m_leg"/>
          <inertial pos="0 0 -0.12" mass="2.2" diaginertia="0.015 0.015 0.003"/>
          <geom name="knee_cap_r" type="sphere" size="0.050" material="joint_mat"/>
          <geom name="shin_r_g" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.032 0 0 -0.335" size="0.049"
                material="skin"/>

          <body name="foot_r" pos="0 0 -0.335">
            <joint name="ank_pitch_r" axis="0 1 0" range="-0.9 0.6" class="m_leg"/>
            <joint name="ank_roll_r" axis="1 0 0" range="-0.4 0.4" class="m_leg"/>
            <inertial pos="0.02 0 -0.03" mass="0.8" diaginertia="0.003 0.004 0.002"/>
            <geom name="foot_r_g" size="0.100 0.046 0.0225" pos="0.024 0 -0.0325" class="m_foot"/>
            <!-- rounded toe cap, so the shoe is not a bare rectangle -->
            <geom name="toe_r" type="ellipsoid" size="0.040 0.045 0.028" pos="0.098 0 -0.028"
                  material="shoe" class="deco"/>
          </body>
        </body>
      </body>

      <body name="hip_l" pos="0 0.105 -0.052">
        <joint name="hip_yaw_l" axis="0 0 1" range="-0.7 0.7" class="m_leg"/>
        <joint name="hip_roll_l" axis="1 0 0" range="-0.5 0.5" class="m_leg"/>
        <joint name="hip_pitch_l" axis="0 1 0" range="-1.9 1.0" class="m_leg"/>
        <inertial pos="0 0 -0.12" mass="3.2" diaginertia="0.022 0.022 0.006"/>
        <geom name="hip_cap_l" type="sphere" size="0.060" material="dress"/>
        <geom name="thigh_l" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.045 0 0 -0.345" size="0.058"
              material="skin"/>

        <body name="shin_l" pos="0 0 -0.345">
          <joint name="knee_l" axis="0 1 0" range="0 2.2" class="m_leg"/>
          <inertial pos="0 0 -0.12" mass="2.2" diaginertia="0.015 0.015 0.003"/>
          <geom name="knee_cap_l" type="sphere" size="0.050" material="joint_mat"/>
          <geom name="shin_l_g" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 -0.032 0 0 -0.335" size="0.049"
                material="skin"/>

          <body name="foot_l" pos="0 0 -0.335">
            <joint name="ank_pitch_l" axis="0 1 0" range="-0.9 0.6" class="m_leg"/>
            <joint name="ank_roll_l" axis="1 0 0" range="-0.4 0.4" class="m_leg"/>
            <inertial pos="0.02 0 -0.03" mass="0.8" diaginertia="0.003 0.004 0.002"/>
            <geom name="foot_l_g" size="0.100 0.046 0.0225" pos="0.024 0 -0.0325" class="m_foot"/>
            <geom name="toe_l" type="ellipsoid" size="0.040 0.045 0.028" pos="0.098 0 -0.028"
                  material="shoe" class="deco"/>
          </body>
        </body>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- One actuator per hand; the two fingers follow each other through this tendon. -->
  <tendon>
    <fixed name="grip_r_t">
      <joint joint="grip_r1" coef="1"/>
      <joint joint="grip_r2" coef="-1"/>
    </fixed>
    <fixed name="grip_l_t">
      <joint joint="grip_l1" coef="1"/>
      <joint joint="grip_l2" coef="-1"/>
    </fixed>
  </tendon>
  <equality>
    <tendon tendon1="grip_r_t"/>
    <tendon tendon1="grip_l_t"/>
  </equality>

  <contact>
    <exclude body1="pelvis" body2="torso"/>
    <exclude body1="pelvis" body2="hip_r"/>
    <exclude body1="pelvis" body2="hip_l"/>
    <exclude body1="torso" body2="shoulder_r"/>
    <exclude body1="torso" body2="shoulder_l"/>
    <exclude body1="torso" body2="head"/>
    <exclude body1="palm_r" body2="finger_r1"/>
    <exclude body1="palm_r" body2="finger_r2"/>
    <exclude body1="palm_l" body2="finger_l1"/>
    <exclude body1="palm_l" body2="finger_l2"/>
  </contact>

  <actuator>
    <!-- neck (2) -->
    <position name="neck_yaw" joint="neck_yaw" ctrlrange="-1.75 1.75" kp="25" kv="2.5"/>
    <!-- Pitch carries the head's weight against gravity, so it needs more than yaw does. -->
    <position name="neck_pitch" joint="neck_pitch" ctrlrange="-0.35 1.05" kp="35" kv="3.0"/>

    <!-- legs (12) -->
    <position name="hip_yaw_r" joint="hip_yaw_r" ctrlrange="-0.7 0.7" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="hip_roll_r" joint="hip_roll_r" ctrlrange="-0.5 0.5" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="hip_pitch_r" joint="hip_pitch_r" ctrlrange="-1.9 1.0" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="knee_r" joint="knee_r" ctrlrange="0 2.2" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="ank_pitch_r" joint="ank_pitch_r" ctrlrange="-0.9 0.6" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="ank_roll_r" joint="ank_roll_r" ctrlrange="-0.4 0.4" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="hip_yaw_l" joint="hip_yaw_l" ctrlrange="-0.7 0.7" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="hip_roll_l" joint="hip_roll_l" ctrlrange="-0.5 0.5" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="hip_pitch_l" joint="hip_pitch_l" ctrlrange="-1.9 1.0" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="knee_l" joint="knee_l" ctrlrange="0 2.2" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="ank_pitch_l" joint="ank_pitch_l" ctrlrange="-0.9 0.6" class="m_leg"/>
    <position name="ank_roll_l" joint="ank_roll_l" ctrlrange="-0.4 0.4" class="m_leg"/>
    <!-- waist (1) -->
    <position name="waist_yaw" joint="waist_yaw" ctrlrange="-1.0 1.0"/>
    <!-- arms (8) -->
    <position name="sh_pitch_r" joint="sh_pitch_r" ctrlrange="-3.1 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
    <position name="sh_roll_r" joint="sh_roll_r" ctrlrange="-1.7 0.35" class="m_arm"/>
    <position name="sh_yaw_r" joint="sh_yaw_r" ctrlrange="-1.6 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
    <position name="elbow_r" joint="elbow_r" ctrlrange="-2.4 0.0" class="m_arm"/>
    <position name="sh_pitch_l" joint="sh_pitch_l" ctrlrange="-3.1 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
    <position name="sh_roll_l" joint="sh_roll_l" ctrlrange="-0.35 1.7" class="m_arm"/>
    <position name="sh_yaw_l" joint="sh_yaw_l" ctrlrange="-1.6 1.6" class="m_arm"/>
    <position name="elbow_l" joint="elbow_l" ctrlrange="-2.4 0.0" class="m_arm"/>
    <!-- grippers (2) -->
    <position name="grip_r" joint="grip_r1" ctrlrange="0 0.9" class="m_finger"/>
    <position name="grip_l" joint="grip_l1" ctrlrange="0 0.9" class="m_finger"/>
  </actuator>

  <keyframe>
    <!--
      "stand": knees slightly bent, arms hanging at the sides.

      The arm pose is deliberate. H1 rests at shoulder pitch -0.25, which holds the arms out
      in front of the body -- fine for a robot about to push a door, but on this silhouette it
      reads as hovering rather than standing, and it hides the skirt. Pitch is nearly zero here
      so the arms hang, with a little outward roll (+/-0.20) to keep the hands off the hips and
      leave a visible gap between arm and body. That gap is what makes the shape legible.

      qpos follows MuJoCo's body-tree order, which is NOT the order the joints are declared in
      the actuator block. Torso and arms come before the legs, and BOTH neck joints come before
      the arms:
        7  free joint (xyz + quat)
        1  waist_yaw
        2  neck_yaw, neck_pitch
        6  right arm  (sh_pitch, sh_roll, sh_yaw, elbow, grip1, grip2)
        6  left arm
        6  right leg  (hip_yaw, hip_roll, hip_pitch, knee, ank_pitch, ank_roll)
        6  left leg
      = 34 values. Pelvis height 0.7496 is solved, not guessed: with the bent knees below, it
      is the height that puts the sole of each foot box exactly on z=0. Measure it from the
      foot geom (centre minus half-size), not from geom_rbound -- rbound is a bounding SPHERE,
      which for a 0.10 x 0.046 x 0.0225 foot box overestimates the drop by nearly 9 cm.
    -->
    <key name="stand"
         qpos="0 0 0.7659  1 0 0 0
               0
               0 0
               -0.05 -0.20 0 -0.22   0 0
               -0.05  0.20 0 -0.22   0 0
               0 0 -0.25 0.5 -0.25 0
               0 0 -0.25 0.5 -0.25 0"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
